# Manager GUI design

The manager GUI shows the projects of the 'single' manager in the 'manager window', and sends the requests the user makes there. The manager, its requests and the terms in quotes that this document does not define are in ManagerDesign.

## The manager GUI and the manager

Each request of the manager window is a message, and the error of a `Refused` shows in a dialog of the manager window.
The manager GUI reads `projects.info` and the project folders itself, and names each registered project by name inference (see Registering a folder in ManagerDesign).
The manager GUI knows which projects are 'busy' from the starts and the ends of the 'mains chains' and of the compilations that the standard input of the manager GUI reports.
The manager GUI selects the project of each `Show` with a project, of each `Register`, and of each request about one project that the standard input of the manager GUI reports, other than the special cases of `EditMetadata`.
Showing the manager window asks the desk to bring the manager window to the front.
Closing the manager window ends the manager GUI.

While the manager window is shown and not minimized, every three seconds the manager GUI runs an 'autoCheck' of the selected project and of one other project, taking the other projects in turn. A project gets an 'autoCheck' of the manager GUI when the project stops being 'busy'.
An 'autoCheck' of the manager GUI reads the project folder as an 'autoCheck' of the manager does, and changes nothing: the manager GUI writes no `_error.txt` and ends no 'mains chain'.
When an 'autoCheck' of the manager GUI finds a 'busy' project 'invalid', 'out of date' or unreachable, the manager GUI sends `Check` of that project: the 'autoCheck' of the manager at the start of that `Check` ends the 'mains chains' (see Files changing while a 'mains chain' runs in ManagerDesign).
The 'autoCheck' every three seconds may keep the outcome of the previous 'autoCheck' of the project when every file and every folder of the project folder has a last modification time before the start of that previous 'autoCheck'.
An 'autoCheck' of the manager GUI that can not reach the project folder shows the 'unreachable dialog' of the project: an error naming the project folder, with the buttons Locate and Forget.
Closing the 'unreachable dialog' is a `Forget` of the project.
Locate opens a folder picker, then sends an `EditMetadata` giving the picked folder as the `path` of the project.
```
A Code project hello has the folder /home/user/hello, and (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.
B The user moves /home/user/hello to /home/user/old/hello.
C An 'autoCheck' of the manager GUI can not reach /home/user/hello.
D The manager GUI shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project hello.
E The user presses Locate.
F The user picks /home/user/old/hello.
G Project hello has the folder /home/user/old/hello, and (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.

A The folders of the projects hello and world are unreachable.
B The manager GUI shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project hello.
C The user presses Forget.
D The manager forgets project hello.
E The manager GUI shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project world.
```

Docs, Base documentation, Browse, Logs and Fearless demos, which open another program on a file or a web page, Info, which opens the Info dialog, the Clear button of an Output, which empties the Output, and Import zip, which writes files and then sends messages, are operations of the manager GUI that send no message. An operation that finds nothing to open says so in a dialog.

## General GUI shape
The messages of dropping files or folders on the manager window are the messages of one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand` (see Starting the 'single' manager in ManagerDesign), for each path dropped.
Every button is disabled for half a second after it is pressed.

A menu bar at the top, with two menus:
```
Manager menu:
  Add folder              `Register`, through a file picker
  Edit project metadata   `EditMetadata`
  Connect Eclipse         `ConnectEclipse`, through a file picker of a file or a folder; after `Applied`, a dialog says so, asking to restart Eclipse if Eclipse is running and to open the Fearless perspective
  Base documentation      `Coordinator.docs` of the package `base`, with any folder
  Info                    the Info dialog
  Reset                   `Reset`, after a confirmation dialog saying what Reset does, with the buttons Reset and Cancel, Cancel preselected
  Quit                    `Quit`
Store menu:
  Fearless demos          opens `https://marcoservetto.github.io/fearless_demos/` with the program that opens web pages
  Import zip              see below
```
The editor of Edit project metadata remembers the content the editor opened; when the user commits, and `projects.info` does not contain that content, the editor refuses the commit in a dialog, and the user edits the new content again; otherwise the commit is an `EditMetadata`.
The Info dialog shows the 'drawing line' of this machine (`Coordinator.drawing`, see CoordinatorDesign; the 'drawing line' is defined in the Implementation notes of ImprovedGuiAPI.txt), with the button Close.

Import zip asks, in a dialog, for the URL of a zip, `https:` or `file:`, whose file name is `<name>_app.zip`, `<name>_edu_app.zip`, `<name>_lib.zip` or `<name>_edu_lib.zip`, where `<name>` starts with a lowercase letter and contains only lowercase letters, digits and `_`; a file name ending with `_edu_app.zip` or `_edu_lib.zip` is a `<name>_edu_app.zip` or a `<name>_edu_lib.zip`. The dialog refuses any other URL, shows that the zip is downloading, and shows the error of a download or of a zip that does not contain what follows. `<zip>` is the file name of the zip without `.zip`.
A `<name>_app.zip` or `<name>_edu_app.zip` contains the marker `<name>.fearless` at the top level of the zip. The import writes, with `fearless_imported_zips` next to the manager folder:
```
<name>_app.zip      the folder fearless_imported_zips/<zip>, containing <zip>.zip, and the marker and the PNG files of .config/icon of the zip
<name>_edu_app.zip  the folder fearless_imported_zips/<zip>, containing the files and the folders of the zip
<name>_lib.zip      the file lib/<zip>.zip of the selected project
<name>_edu_lib.zip  the folder lib/<zip> of the selected project, containing the files and the folders of the zip
```
Then the import sends `Register` of `fearless_imported_zips/<zip>`, and `Kind` code and `Compile` of the registered project. With no selected project, the dialog refuses a `<name>_lib.zip` or `<name>_edu_lib.zip`, asking to select a project first.
When the folder or the file the import writes exists already, the import asks whether to replace that folder or that file, and sends `Terminate` of the project of that folder or that file before writing.

The 'desktop with icons' fills the manager window under the menu bar while no project is selected; while a project is selected, the 'desktop with icons' takes the left side of a vertical divider, and the project area takes the right side.
The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no PNG file in `.config/icon`, an icon generated from the project name.
Tiles represent the registered projects, sorted by name: each tile shows the project icon with the state badge of the project, and the project name under the project icon.
A 'busy' project shows an animated badge.
Clicking a tile selects the project of the tile.
Double clicking the tile of a project in the state `code: compiled` is `Run` of that project; for a project with one main, the `Run` comes after a `SelectMains` naming that main. For any other project, and for a project with no main, or with several mains and no selected main, a double click is a click.

## Project area


Top to bottom:
```
header          every project
selected mains  a project with selected mains that shows no Run V
shortcut files  a code project with a shortcut file in the top level of the project folder
Output          every project
Tests           a code project
Attenuators     a code project, a project with attenuators
Extensions      a code project whose mains claim extensions with OpenWith
Data            a code project, a data project, a project with mounts
Logs            a code project
```

Header, on one line: the project name, and the following buttons:
- The main button, the first of these that holds:
  - Terminate, while the project is 'busy';
  - Check, for a project that is not code, and for an 'invalid' code project;
  - Compile, for an 'out of date' code project;
  - Run, for a code project with one main: a `SelectMains` naming that main, then `Run` of the project; Run V (V an arrow down), for a code project with several mains; Run greyed out, for a code project with no main.
- The Docs V button (enabled once the mains are known), opening a menu of the packages of the project, the `packages` of `State.compiled` (see CoordinatorDesign); choosing a package opens the documentation of that package (`Coordinator.docs`) with the program that opens `.html` files, which is the manager while the manager 'holds' `.html` (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign).
- The Kind V button, showing the kind of the project, like `Kind: code`, and opening a menu with one entry for each kind the project is not: "Back to idle", "Become dataRead", "Become dataEdit" and "Become code".
- The Project V button, opening a menu with Browse, opening the project folder in the file explorer, Forget (`Forget`), Delete cache (`DeleteCache`), and Fix (`Fix`, after a confirmation dialog saying what Fix does, with the buttons Fix and Cancel, Cancel preselected).
While the project is 'busy', every GUI operation of the project area that is a request about the project, other than `Terminate`, is greyed out.

Pressing Run V opens a drop-down menu showing the mains of the project and the selected mains that are not mains of the project, each with a checkbox, the selected mains checked, and a Run button at the end of the list, disabled while no entry is checked or a checked entry is not a main of the project. Checking or unchecking an entry is a `SelectMains` naming the checked entries. That Run button is `Run` of the project.

Selected mains: the selected mains, with a Remove button: a `SelectMains` naming no main.

Shortcut files: for each `Shortcut` claim of a main of the project whose shortcut file (see Shortcut files in OpenWith and Shortcut in ManagerDesign) is in the top level of the project folder, the icon of the `Shortcut`, with the name of the shortcut file under the icon.
Double clicking a shortcut file there is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with the path of that shortcut file.

A 'collapsible area' has a title and a body: clicking the title hides or shows the body; the body has a vertical scroll bar and is at most half the height of the manager window.
Each part from the Output to the Logs is a 'collapsible area' with that part as title.

Output: a Clear button on the title. The Output shows what the requests about the project write in the Output, as the standard input of the manager GUI reports it. The Output keeps everything until the Output is cleared, or until a well formed request that writes in the Output of the project starts.

Tests: a tree, as the last 'autoCheck' of the project found the unit test logs (`.out/logs/_base/unit_test_log$<time>.log`):
- one top level node for each unit test log written since the last 'mains chain' of the project started, in the order the logs were written, or, before any such start since the manager GUI started, for the newest unit test log; the node names the main of that 'mains chain' that was running when the log was written (or, with no such main, the time of the log), with the counts, like `hello.MyTests: 3 passed, 1 failed, 0 disabled`, and a green mark, or a red mark when a test case failed;
- under each top level node, one node per test case of the log, with the name of the test case and a green, red or grey mark for passed, failed or disabled;
- under a failed test case, the lines of the text of the failure.
A top level node with a failed test case, and a failed test case, start expanded; the other nodes start collapsed.

Attenuators: one block for each main of the project and for each main that `attenuators` names, sorted by name: the attenuators applied to that main in order, each with Remove, Up and Down buttons, then a drop down of the attenuators of the project, with an Add button. Every change is an `Attenuate` of that main.
```
A (greet.NoGui) in hello.greet.Hello.attenuators.
B The user renames the type greet.NoGui to greet.NoWindow in the code of project hello.
C The user compiles project hello.
D The Attenuators area shows greet.NoGui on greet.Hello, marked as not in the project.
```

Extensions: one block per main with an `OpenWith` claim:
  "Extensions this main opens:" (the extension of each `OpenWith` claim of that main, with its icon),
  "Conflicting extensions:" (each of those extensions another main also claims, naming that main).

Data: see DataReadDataEditDesign.

Logs: the log files the mains wrote in `.out/logs`, with a Logs button opening that folder in the file explorer.

Every entry of `projects.info` about a project except `path` shows in the project area of that project, and every entry except `kind` has a GUI operation that removes that entry.

## Future design

What the design will cover later; nothing in this section is current behaviour.
- The manager GUI is a Fearless program of the standard library: it sends its messages with `CapManager`, and reads its standard input with an API of the standard library still to be designed.
- The Output keeps only the last part of what a running main or a compilation prints.
- Removing or changing a data project mounted on a DataRead type of a code project shows a confirmation dialog in the manager GUI, naming the code projects that get a broken mount.

## Implementation notes

- The manager GUI is a Java program of Controllers, in a JVM of its own; the 'single' manager passes it the paths of the manager folder and of the base cache.
- On macOS the manager GUI shows no Dock icon of its own (`apple.awt.UIElement`): the Dock icon is the one of the manager (see Tray in ManagerDesign).
- A desk, such as GNOME, may refuse to bring the manager window to the front, and tell the user in its own way, for example with a notification that Fearless Manager is ready.
